       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order as written. Confirming it doesn't change the numbers, and I can't verify the claim about the label. The FDA-approved metformin labeling gives a maximum of 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release tablets in adults. Some extended-release products have a lower maximum. An order of 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, which is double that limit. Metformin at this level also raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the labeled maximum)\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals** (2,000 mg/day)\n- **Metformin 1,700 mg PO once daily**, or another dose within the labeled range\n\nIf you think this patient needs more than the labeled maximum, the order needs to go through your pharmacy or the attending's own process. Tell me which of the options above you want and I'll enter it."
      }
